<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long Island Sound</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Long Island Sound is a tidal estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and Long Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, about 180 km long. Its northern shore was settled by English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> from the 1630s, who founded New Haven and other coastal towns. It has since been a busy waterway for shipping, fishing and recreation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-island-sound.jpg" alt="Long Island Sound" width="316" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Decumanus at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wpdms_ev26188_long_island_sound.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Long Island Sound is a marine sound and tidal estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. It lies chiefly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> to the north and Long Island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to the south, and from west to east it stretches 110 miles (180 km) from the East River and the Throgs Neck Bridge in New York City, along the North Shore of Long Island, to Block Island Sound. It forms part of the Intracoastal Waterway. It is 21 miles (34 km) across at its widest and varies in depth from 65 to 230 feet (20 to 70 m), a mix of fresh water from tributaries and salt water from the Atlantic.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>'s major cities on the sound include Stamford, Norwalk, Bridgeport, New Haven and New London, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> side lie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">Rye</a>, Glen Cove, New Rochelle, Oyster Bay, Port Jefferson and parts of Queens and the Bronx. Rivers that empty into it include the Connecticut, Housatonic and Thames. The whole watershed held about 8.93 million people at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>About 18,000 years ago <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> and much of Long Island lay under the Late Wisconsin Glacier, an ice sheet about 1,000 metres thick in its interior, when sea level was about 100 metres lower than today. The Sound formed when the terminal moraine that dammed glacial Lake Connecticut failed and sea water mixed with the lake's fresh water. Before colonisation an estimated 10,000 to 15,000 native people lived along its shores.</p><p>The first European to record its existence was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navigator Adriaen Block, who entered it from the East River in 1614. In colonial times it was known as the Devil's Belt, and the reefs across it as the Devil's Stepping Stones, which gave Stepping Stones Lighthouse its name.</p><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> grew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> was used for manufacturing such as textiles and metal finishing, as well as fishing and oyster harvesting, and the growth brought pollution, including mercury discharged by the hatting industry in Danbury. From the 1950s and 1960s the federal government recognised the effects on water quality and health. After the Clean Water Act of 1972, the Environmental Protection Agency joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1985 to establish the Long Island Sound Study, with plans for restoration and clean-up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ferries link Long Island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, notably the Bridgeport and Port Jefferson Ferry and the Cross Sound Ferry between Orient Point and New London, carrying vehicles and foot passengers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">The Sound</a> has long supported commercial and recreational fishing for oysters, lobsters, scallops, flounder, striped bass and bluefish. Underwater cables carry electricity beneath it, and bridges and a tunnel have been proposed over the years; no crossing has been built since the Throgs Neck Bridge in the early 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Long Island Sound” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1638 the London minister John Davenport and the merchant Theophilus Eaton led a wealthy company of Puritans to Quinnipiac on <b>Long Island Sound</b>, buying land from the local people.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-haven-colony">New Haven Colony</a></li></ul></section>
